1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,320
I am the Time Chronicler crossing a thousand years of dust and darkness to tell you the true lost history of Nalanda in Bihar today.

2
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:21,400
Imagine that vast library packed with palm-leaf manuscripts where once a fire started and the raging flames did not stop for three full months.

3
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:31,800
Nearly nine million palm-leaf manuscripts with a thousand years of scholarly effort turned into piles of grey ash in a single tragic disaster.

4
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:41,720
This is no invented fairy tale but the proven true story of Nalanda standing in red brick on the ancient red soil of Bihar in India.

5
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:53,000
Whose set fire turned the radiant lighthouse of the first university on earth into silent ash and drifting smoke in a single night in Bihar?

6
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:04,160
Come hold my hand and pass through the golden tunnel of time back thirteen hundred years to the green ancient soil of Magadha in Bihar.

7
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:13,880
If you visit Nalanda in Bihar today you will see only red-brick ruins with broken stupas and empty courtyards under light morning mist.

8
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:22,920
Yet believe this truth that these silent courtyards once held ten thousand students and two thousand teachers studying together daily.

9
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:33,280
Thirsty seekers from China, Korea, Tibet and Persia walked long dusty roads for many months until they reached the ancient gates of Nalanda.

10
00:01:33,280 --> 00:01:42,000
It was the Oxford and Harvard of the ancient world combined where Indian and foreign scholars exchanged manuscripts under banyan shade.

11
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:52,080
Many think university means a British gift like Calcutta or Bombay of 1857, but in the judgment of history this idea is fully wrong.

12
00:01:52,080 --> 00:02:01,880
This idea is fully wrong because the lamp of Nalanda was already burning bright in India seven hundred years before Oxford was even born.

13
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:12,880
In 427 CE Gupta emperor Kumaragupta the First placed the first foundation brick at golden sunrise amid chanting priests and conch shells.

14
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:23,600
Then for nearly eight hundred years through changing seasons and kings, study here continued daily from dawn lamps to midnight without break.

15
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:32,040
So what did the birth moment of this great institution in Magadha look like and whose donation first opened its huge wooden gates?

16
00:02:32,040 --> 00:02:41,240
Nalanda was never only a prayer hall for Buddhist monks but a full university where physicians and astronomers sat side by side daily.

17
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:51,600
Here besides Buddhist philosophy, medicine, astronomy, logic, Sanskrit grammar and mathematics were taught in ink-scented classrooms daily.

18
00:02:51,600 --> 00:03:00,920
Masters here passed Aryabhata's wonder of zero and planetary reckoning to the next generation by drawing chalk dots on stone slates.

19
00:03:00,920 --> 00:03:10,480
The heavy wooden gates of Nalanda stayed open for students of every faith while foreign travellers entered wearing garlands with respect.

20
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:19,600
The most astonishing truth is that study at Nalanda was completely free, a promise that widened the eyes of every newcomer with joy.

21
00:03:19,600 --> 00:03:28,360
Lodging, warm rice meals and herbal medicine were all given by the university while hundreds of students sat together in long dining halls.

22
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:38,520
This vast yearly expense came from the revenue of nearly two hundred donated villages gifted with devotion by Gupta kings and rich merchants.

23
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:47,360
Yet admission was formidably tough as nervous applicants sweated before the main gate thinking of the stern test of the gatekeepers.

24
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:55,960
At the main gate scholar gatekeepers tested each traveller with subtle questions from scrolls while a hundred hopefuls waited with bundles.

25
00:03:55,960 --> 00:04:04,560
Only two or three out of ten could step inside as the chosen hugged each other in delight while the rest returned with heavy hearts.

26
00:04:04,560 --> 00:04:15,920
The soul of Nalanda lived in three towering libraries called Ratnasagara, Ratnodadhi and Ratnaranjaka under golden afternoon sunlight.

27
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:25,480
Among them Ratnodadhi rose nine storeys high in brick and timber with steep stairs climbed daily by monks carrying bundles for wisdom.

28
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:35,320
Row upon row of wooden shelves held millions of palm-leaf manuscripts on philosophy, grammar, medicine and astronomy under lamplight.

29
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:43,760
Scholars estimate this collection equalled nearly nine million volumes, a vast number that exhausted the counting beads of the elders.

30
00:04:43,760 --> 00:04:52,480
It was the greatest treasure house of knowledge in human history whose golden mountain of manuscripts spread light across long centuries.

31
00:04:52,480 --> 00:05:03,040
I am Xuanzang the Buddhist pilgrim from distant China standing in brown robes at the ancient gates of Nalanda to bow humbly to history.

32
00:05:03,040 --> 00:05:14,200
Across scorching deserts and frozen mountain passes bearing thirst and blizzards I finally set foot on the holy soil of Nalanda in 630 CE.

33
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:24,520
I remained at Nalanda for five continuous years and received instruction in Yogachara philosophy from Master Shilabhadra in a lamp-lit manuscript study hall.

34
00:05:24,520 --> 00:05:35,680
At sunrise I left Nalanda with a caravan of horses and porters carrying six hundred fifty-seven sacred manuscripts and Buddha images tied carefully in cloth bundles.

35
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:45,520
The outer world first learned the glory of Nalanda from my written travel records, which reached Chinese translators and were kept safely in distant archives.

36
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:56,520
Examinations at Nalanda took the form of debate contests in the stone courtyard, where two scholars advanced after the conch call while hundreds sat around to listen.

37
00:05:56,520 --> 00:06:08,320
In the courtyard under midday sun two scholars presented sharp logical arguments, while hundreds holding palm notes listened silently to each statement with attention.

38
00:06:08,320 --> 00:06:18,240
A defeated scholar sometimes garlanded the victor with flowers and accepted his discipleship with humility, which all present witnessed with bowed heads and respect.

39
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:30,720
In logic, dialectics and astronomy the masters of Nalanda were regarded as foremost, for foreign guests observed the hand-drawn star charts kept on wooden tables.

40
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:41,320
A palm-leaf degree from Nalanda was valued as a golden key to honour across Asia, through which graduates obtained teaching opportunities in monasteries and courts.

41
00:06:41,320 --> 00:06:55,040
I am Atisha Dipankara, born in a lotus-pond and coconut-palm village of Vikrampur in Bengal, where in childhood I learned the first lessons of compassion from my mother.

42
00:06:55,040 --> 00:07:05,400
I served as master at both Nalanda and Vikramashila, where each day I taught compassion and wisdom to rows of monks from a stone teaching seat in the hall.

43
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:16,640
In ten forty-two I journeyed for religious reform across the snow-covered Himalayan passes with disciples at the invitation of the Tibetan king in my advanced age.

44
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:28,520
In Tibetan mountain monasteries monks bow before my portrait with butter lamps and incense sticks, a ritual kept through successive generations of disciples.

45
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:39,800
This son of Bengal carried the lamp of Nalanda wisdom beyond the Himalayas, from whose preserved manuscripts morning lessons are conducted daily in Tibetan monasteries.

46
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:51,560
At dawn amid light mist the courtyards of Nalanda awoke to bells and recitation murmurs, where thousands of monks began the day in orderly rows beneath brick temples.

47
00:07:51,560 --> 00:08:02,880
As the dawn bell rang rows of monks settled into meditation in the pillared prayer hall, while golden sunlight through window gaps cast a glow upon their ochre robes.

48
00:08:02,880 --> 00:08:14,040
Then sessions of lessons, scriptural debate and logic contests began, where scribes seated at wooden desks copied palm-leaf manuscripts attentively in daylight.

49
00:08:14,040 --> 00:08:24,560
At midday monks in long rows humbly accepted food with clay begging bowls, where measured portions of rice and lentils taught lessons of restraint and contentment.

50
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:36,440
In the evening study circles gathered again under oil-lamp light, where teachers and pupils seated on wooden platforms revised lessons and chanted verses in unison.

51
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:48,920
Students from Korea, Java, Tibet and Persia lived in the same courtyards, where despite different tongues exchanging manuscripts built close bonds among them.

52
00:08:48,920 --> 00:09:01,840
This gathering of many lands, many tongues and varied robes made Nalanda an international learning centre ahead of its age, as recorded in stone inscriptions and notes.

53
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:11,360
By the twelfth century crisis gathered over Nalanda like dark clouds, when cracks appeared on old stupas and the number of resident monks steadily declined.

54
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:22,360
As royal patronage from the Pala kings declined and treasury reserves dwindled, nearby Vikramashila gained prominence as the new favoured royal centre of learning.

55
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:32,760
Even with revenue from two hundred endowed villages it became increasingly hard to meet the regular costs of rice, lentils and robes for the vast monastery and guests.

56
00:09:32,760 --> 00:09:43,640
At the same time political unrest, wars and power struggles increased across northern India, making trade routes and pilgrim roads unsafe for ordinary travellers.

57
00:09:43,640 --> 00:09:54,520
In dark corridors the lamp of learning flickered in stormy winds, while war drums heard from afar announced signs of a terrible assault soon to come.

58
00:09:54,520 --> 00:10:04,800
We now speak of the campaign era linked to the forces of Bakhtiyar Khalji in a calm historical view, maintaining full dignity and restraint without any hatred.

59
00:10:04,800 --> 00:10:15,120
The Persian chronicle Tabaqat-i-Nasiri records that sustained military campaigns took place across Bihar between 1193 and 1200 CE.

60
00:10:15,120 --> 00:10:26,040
Historians explain that soldiers from afar mistook the high-walled monastery for a fortified fort, and from that misunderstanding an armed clash began near the gate.

61
00:10:26,040 --> 00:10:37,360
The monks of Nalanda were wholly unarmed seekers of wisdom devoted to scriptures and compassion, and to their memory we offer sincere respectful homage in silence.

62
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:48,960
I am an aged monk who survived those dreadful days, standing in the shadow of the burning library to bear witness to true events seen with my own eyes.

63
00:10:48,960 --> 00:11:01,400
Suddenly I saw raging flames spread through the nine-storey Ratnodadhi library building, while sparks flying in stormy winds filled the night sky with reddish light.

64
00:11:01,400 --> 00:11:12,680
Millions of palm-leaf manuscripts, wooden racks and ghee lamps burned together, so towering columns of black smoke covered the sky and drifted in winds for many weeks.

65
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:22,000
Folk memory says the Ratnodadhi library fire smouldered for nearly three months. Wooden racks and tightly packed palm leaves together kept the blaze alive.

66
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:33,080
A few monks tied manuscript bundles on their shoulders and fled at night through dense forest trails. They risked their lives to carry those texts toward Nepal and Tibet.

67
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:46,680
Black ash of burnt palm leaves still clings to the old monk's trembling hands. Memories of his lost teachers and fellow monks give him silent pain daily.

68
00:11:46,680 --> 00:11:56,840
A folk tale says a physician cured a sick commander with medicine coated on manuscript pages. Some claim anger over that episode caused the library burning.

69
00:11:56,840 --> 00:12:08,120
But before accepting this startling tale we must pause and verify the facts. In history verified evidence always holds far more value than emotion.

70
00:12:08,120 --> 00:12:18,960
Crucially the contemporary Tabaqat-i-Nasiri carries no such healing story at all. It describes the Bihar campaigns but mentions no miraculous cure anywhere.

71
00:12:18,960 --> 00:12:29,360
Modern historians therefore treat this story as a later folk legend only. Colourful exaggeration joined the tale along its long oral journey.

72
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:40,880
With full respect for every community we say, whatever the truth, the loss was irreplaceable. This terrible ruin of knowledge was humanity's shared grief.

73
00:12:40,880 --> 00:12:52,920
Even after terrible ruin Nalanda did not vanish fully. A few monks stayed among broken roofs and fallen stupas. They held daily lessons from rescued manuscripts.

74
00:12:52,920 --> 00:13:05,480
In 1235 the Tibetan traveller Dharmaswamin crossed hard roads to reach the broken monastery. He saw ninety-year-old Rahulashribhadra sincerely teaching seventy pupils.

75
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:16,120
In broken rooms his devoted pupils guarded rescued manuscripts against their chests. Study circles never stopped daily despite scarce light and meagre food.

76
00:13:16,120 --> 00:13:27,320
Slowly the surviving monks scattered toward Nepal, Tibet and Southeast Asia. Nalanda's learning travelled with them and took root in new centres.

77
00:13:27,320 --> 00:13:37,760
After that Nalanda lay buried under soil for many long centuries. Grass, peepal trees and farmers' ploughs kept moving above it year after year.

78
00:13:37,760 --> 00:13:47,680
In the 1860s British archaeologist Alexander Cunningham surveyed these huge mounds. He identified them as the buried remains of ancient Nalanda.

79
00:13:47,680 --> 00:13:58,600
Later excavations unearthed monasteries, temples, bronze statues and clay seals. Diggers also found countless burnt manuscript pieces and ash layers.

80
00:13:58,600 --> 00:14:07,840
World recognition for this rare discovery arrived in the year 2016. UNESCO then declared Nalanda a World Heritage Site of global value.

81
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:18,600
Thus the world's first residential university rose again from the soil. Its ancient brick stairs and temples reappeared in the sunrise glow.

82
00:14:18,600 --> 00:14:28,240
The long story did not end among the silent ruins. Beside the old foundations a fresh chapter began with students walking a modern campus.

83
00:14:28,240 --> 00:14:37,680
In 2014 the new Nalanda University opened its doors beside the ruins. India and many Asian nations supported it with funds and teachers.

84
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:47,280
Today students from many lands gather on that campus again. Bright classrooms host lively debates and readings of ancient manuscripts.

85
00:14:47,280 --> 00:14:57,120
After nearly eight hundred years the extinguished lamp burns brightly again. Its warm light spreads clear hope across young faces today.

86
00:14:57,120 --> 00:15:07,400
History gave its final proof: books can burn, but thirst can never burn. The human craving for knowledge outlives each fire it faces.

87
00:15:07,400 --> 00:15:18,200
Think once: vast stores of manuscripts and timber burned there together. That is why Nalanda's fire reportedly burned for nearly three months.

88
00:15:18,200 --> 00:15:28,040
There lay nearly a thousand years of dreams, discipline and labour. A single day's fire could not reduce that vast reserve to ash.

89
00:15:28,040 --> 00:15:38,440
Though manuscripts turned to ash, knowledge lives on in human minds. Nalanda's lessons echo in Tibetan chants and Bengal Baul songs.

90
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:49,960
Now we ask your view: what best protects knowledge in this digital age. Scanned copies, translations or open online archives, which do you pick.

91
